  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday night through Tuesday)\nIssued at 252 AM EDT Wed Oct 28 2026\n\nThe extended periods start off with continued dry weather but things \nwill be changing as we get into the weekend. By Thu night the high \npressure ridge will still be mostly in place centered over eastern \nTN/western NC. This ridge will still be strong enough to keep the \narea precipitation free through Fri but it is weakening. By Fri \nmorning the remnants from Tropical Storm Vance will have moved \nonshore the west coast of Mexico and into Western TX. This wave of \ntropical moisture is expected to spread North and East across the \ncentral Plains and the Gulf Coast states (TX/LA/MS/AL) through Sat \nmorning. By daybreak Sat the ridge center that was keeping us dry \nwill have moved NE into the New England states with a weak wedge \nsetup down the Eastern seaboard. The models begin to show some of \nthe tropical moisture to our west beginning to push into West GA by \nlate Sat afternoon/evening. One issue is timing with the \nintroduction of the tropical moisture across our area as things are \nstill a bit muddy. The GFS and ECMWF booth agree that this next \nsystem is coming but they show its earliest arrival by 18z Sat and \nits latest by 06z Sun. Once this system/moisture is moves into the \nstate it is expected to continue pushing east exiting the area Mon \nafternoon/evening. Best precip chances still appear to be from the \nearly morning hours Sun to Sun evening/night. Right now not seeing \nmuch in the way of severe weather with this system but it does appear \nit could be a very efficient rain maker. Looking at most locations \nseeing 1\" to 2\" possible with some isolated higher amounts through \nMon.\n\nTemperatures in the extended periods begin a bit warm with highs \nexpected in the upper 70s to lower 80s. With increased clouds Sat \nand precip moving in Sun Temps decrease a bit with highs Sat mainly \nin the 70s to near 80 and highs in the 60s and 70s Sun. Low temps \nexpected mainly in the 40s and 50s through day 7.",
